messages:
jules_customer: hey @support — hitting an intermittent failure with structured JSON mode over streaming. our parser throws JSONDecodeError ~30% of the time. attached is a raw capture from one failed session. Can someone take a look?

ana_support: sorry to hear that — can you paste the raw response bytes (the exact chunk boundaries) and the request_id/x-request-id from the response headers? also which model + client lib are you using?

jules_customer: sure. model: gpt-neox-20b-v1-stream, using our node client (fetch -> response.body.getReader). request id header: req_0a1b2c3d. raw chunks below (exact byte boundaries):

--- raw chunks start ---
chunk A: {"user_id":"u_123","events":[{"type":"msg","text":"hi"}, {"type":"msg","text":"here
are some lines"}]}
chunk B: ,"meta":{"ts":"2026-03-17T12:34:56Z","cursor":"eyJ2Ijox"}
chunk C: ,"extra":{"items":[{"id":1,"value":"ok"}               

--- raw chunks end (note trailing spaces/newline) ---

when we try to JSON.parse on the incremental buffer on each chunk boundary we sometimes see the second chunk starting with a comma which confuses our incremental parser and sometimes the last chunk gets an early disconnect so the final closing brackets never arrive. the error is: 'Unterminated array starting at position...'

ingest-bot: captured headers: x-request-id=req_0a1b2c3d, x-runtime-node=rw-6, model=gpt-neox-20b-v1-stream, stream=true

maya_engineer: thanks — two quick clarifications: (1) are you attempting to parse each chunk individually as a standalone JSON document? (2) does your client treat each chunk as an event (ndjson) or as pieces of one large JSON payload?

jules_customer: we were treating chunks as independent events (i.e., JSON.parse per chunk). previously other providers gave newline-delimited JSON so that worked. here the server is sending a single top-level JSON object split across TCP chunks, so per-chunk parse fails. we switched to buffering+try-parse but still seeing intermittent 'early stop' cases where connection cut before last chunk.

tom_runtime: two likely causes: (A) client-side parser strategy mismatch (assuming ndjson vs top-level streamed object) or (B) server-side early connection close / token-limit enforcement that truncates output mid-object. we should collect the connection close reason and runtime logs for req_0a1b2c3d.

ana_support: for immediate mitigation, can you (a) switch to non-streaming call (stream: false) to validate the model output end-to-end, and (b) if streaming is required, switch to a buffered parser that concatenates chunks until you detect a complete JSON value (e.g., brace counting or use a streaming JSON parser like 'clarinet' or 'JSONStream'). also consider asking the API for ndjson output if you want per-event JSON objects.

jules_customer: ok switching to sync call reproduces valid JSON reliably (so model output appears correct when not interrupted). we still need streaming for latency. any server-side config to help?

maya_engineer: runtime team can enable a 'chunk-safe' header that enforces emitting complete top-level objects per chunk (or switch to NDJSON mode). I'll file a follow-up with the runtime owners and include request id. tom, can you pull the runtime logs for req_0a1b2c3d and check for abrupt disconnects or token-limit truncation?

tom_runtime: taking this. preliminary logs show a connection reset by peer at 2026-03-17T12:34:57. there's also a note about 'client read timeout' at the load balancer. possible that our LB or customer's network closed connection; need HAR/pcap from the customer. also looking at the model-side token budget — there was a soft cap at 2048 tokens for that instance yesterday.

jules_customer: I can share a HAR and the exact fetch code. here's a minimal repro (node):
```js
const res = await fetch('https://api.redwood.ai/v1/streams', {
  method: 'POST',
  headers: { 'Authorization': 'Bearer ...', 'Content-Type': 'application/json' },
  body: JSON.stringify({ model: 'gpt-neox-20b-v1-stream', stream: true, prompt: '...' })
});
const reader = res.body.getReader();
let done=false;
let buf='';
while(!done){
  const { value, done: d } = await reader.read();
  done = d;
  if (value) { buf += new TextDecoder().decode(value); try{ const obj = JSON.parse(buf); console.log('got', obj); buf=''; }catch(e){ /* wait for more */ } }
}
```

this approach works but fails when the connection closes mid-object — we never get a final chunk so the parse never succeeds. is there a recommended header to ask for a final zero-length chunk or end-of-stream marker?

maya_engineer: no special header currently, but best practice is to emit NDJSON (one JSON object per line) for event streams or ensure the client has a retry/fallback on unexpected EOF: either buffer + retry non-streaming mode using same request id, or call the API again to re-request the remaining JSON with conversation context. we'll propose adding an end-of-stream sentinel in the runtime.

ana_support: action items: (1) jules to upload HAR + repro code + indicate whether LB sits in front of client (due EOD PST) — we'll attach to ticket SUP-1429. (2) tom to pull full runtime/logs for req_0a1b2c3d and confirm whether the close was client LB or our runtime (due 4h). (3) maya to open a runtime feature request to support NDJSON or end-of-stream sentinel and estimate work.

jules_customer: acknowledged. attaching HAR now. small note: we also saw intermittent auth 401 on retries, could be a transient token refresh race — included in HAR.

ingest-bot: ticket created SUP-1429, linked req_0a1b2c3d, labels: streaming-json, customer-impact, high-priority

tom_runtime: got the logs, looks like the LB (customer side) dropped idle connections on long-tail streams. runtime didn't intentionally truncate. closing the loop: recommend increasing client keepalive and switching to NDJSON on our side so each event is self-contained. also we'll add better guidance in docs for robust streaming parsers.

ana_support: thanks everyone. jules — once you increase keepalive and test ndjson workaround, pls update SUP-1429. if you still see truncation we will collect a tcpdump from the client's network and escalate to infra. :thumbsup:

jules_customer: will do, thanks for the quick triage!"
